window.LESSONS = [
{subject:'Physics',title:'Motion in a straight line',keywords:'velocity acceleration displacement',body:'<p>Displacement is the change in position; distance is the total path length. Average velocity is displacement divided by elapsed time. Average speed is total distance divided by elapsed time.</p><div class="formula">v = u + at &nbsp; · &nbsp; s = ut + ½at²</div><p>These equations apply when acceleration is constant. Here u is initial velocity, v final velocity, a acceleration, t time and s displacement.</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>A body starts from rest with constant acceleration 2 m/s² for 3 s. Its final velocity is 6 m/s and displacement is 9 m.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>A round trip can have zero displacement and nonzero distance. Average velocity is then zero.</p>'},
{subject:'Physics',title:'Newton’s laws of motion',keywords:'force mass inertia momentum',body:'<p>The first law describes inertia: an object remains at rest or moves with constant velocity unless a net external force acts on it.</p><div class="formula">F<sub>net</sub> = ma</div><p>For constant mass, net force equals mass times acceleration. Force and acceleration are vectors. The SI unit of force is the newton (N).</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>A 3 kg object under a net force of 12 N accelerates at 4 m/s².</p><h3>Third law</h3><p>Interaction forces occur in equal and opposite pairs on different bodies. They do not cancel on a single body’s free-body diagram.</p>'},
{subject:'Physics',title:'Current and resistance',keywords:'ohm voltage electricity circuit',body:'<p>Current is the rate of flow of charge: I = Q/t for a steady current. A potential difference can drive current through a conductor.</p><div class="formula">V = IR &nbsp; · &nbsp; P = VI</div><p>Ohm’s law applies to an ohmic conductor with its physical conditions, including temperature, constant. Resistance is measured in ohms (Ω).</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>A 6 Ω resistor across 12 V carries 2 A. It dissipates 24 W.</p><h3>Connections</h3><p>Resistances add in series. In parallel, reciprocals add: 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂.</p>'},
{subject:'Chemistry',title:'The mole concept',keywords:'avogadro molar mass particles',body:'<p>A mole contains exactly 6.02214076 × 10²³ specified entities. Always identify the entities: atoms, molecules, ions or formula units.</p><div class="formula">n = mass / molar mass</div><p>Use matching mass units. If mass is in grams, use molar mass in g/mol.</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>Using a molar mass of 18 g/mol, 36 g of water is 2 mol of water molecules, approximately 1.204 × 10²⁴ molecules.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>Each H₂O molecule contains three atoms. Two moles of water molecules therefore contain six moles of atoms.</p>'},
{subject:'Chemistry',title:'Chemical bonding basics',keywords:'ionic covalent electron bond',body:'<p>An ionic bond is the electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions. A covalent bond involves shared electron pairs.</p><h3>Examples</h3><p>Sodium chloride forms an ionic crystal lattice. The H₂ molecule contains a covalent bond formed by a shared pair of electrons.</p><h3>Polarity</h3><p>Electronegativity differences influence bond polarity. Molecular polarity also depends on shape: CO₂ has polar bonds but is a nonpolar molecule because its bond dipoles cancel in its linear geometry.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>“Polar bonds” and “polar molecule” are different descriptions.</p>'},
{subject:'Chemistry',title:'Acids, bases and pH',keywords:'hydrogen hydroxide neutral solution',body:'<p>In the Brønsted–Lowry description, an acid donates a proton and a base accepts a proton.</p><div class="formula">pH ≈ −log₁₀[H⁺]</div><p>This concentration expression approximates pH for dilute aqueous solutions; the rigorous definition uses hydrogen-ion activity.</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>If [H⁺] is 10⁻³ mol/L in a dilute solution, pH is approximately 3.</p><h3>Temperature matters</h3><p>In water at 25 °C, neutral pH is approximately 7 and pH + pOH is approximately 14. Neutral pH changes with temperature.</p>'},
{subject:'Biology',title:'Cell structure and organelles',keywords:'mitochondria ribosome nucleus cell',body:'<p>The cell is the basic structural and functional unit of life. Prokaryotic cells lack a membrane-bound nucleus. Eukaryotic cells have a membrane-bound nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles.</p><h3>Key functions</h3><p>Ribosomes synthesize proteins. Mitochondria carry out much of aerobic cellular respiration and ATP production in eukaryotic cells. Chloroplasts carry out photosynthesis in plant and algal cells that contain them.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>Ribosomes are not membrane-bound and occur in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells. Mature mammalian red blood cells lack nuclei and mitochondria.</p>'},
{subject:'Biology',title:'Mendelian inheritance',keywords:'genetics dominant recessive genotype',body:'<p>In a simple Mendelian model, alleles segregate during gamete formation. A dominant allele determines the heterozygote phenotype under complete dominance.</p><h3>Monohybrid cross</h3><div class="formula">Aa × Aa → 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa</div><p>With complete dominance, the corresponding phenotype ratio is 3 dominant : 1 recessive. This assumes normal segregation, random fertilization and equal viability.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>Dominant does not mean more common or better. Incomplete dominance and codominance do not produce this 3:1 phenotype ratio.</p>'},
{subject:'Biology',title:'Photosynthesis foundations',keywords:'plant chlorophyll oxygen light carbon dioxide',body:'<p>Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy. In plants, light-dependent reactions take place in the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts.</p><h3>Two linked processes</h3><p>The light reactions produce ATP and NADPH and release oxygen from water. The Calvin cycle occurs in the stroma and uses ATP and NADPH to help fix carbon dioxide into organic compounds.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>The oxygen released during plant photosynthesis comes from water, not carbon dioxide. The Calvin cycle does not directly use light, but depends on products and regulation linked to the light reactions.</p>'}
];
