LESSONS.push(...[{"subject": "Physics", "grade": 11, "title": "Work, energy and power", "chapter": "Work, energy and power", "keywords": "work kinetic potential energy", "body": "<h3>Core idea</h3><p>Work by a constant force is W = Fs cos θ. Kinetic energy is K = ½mv². The net work done on a body equals its change in kinetic energy.</p><h3>Worked connection</h3><p>A 2 kg body moving at 3 m/s has kinetic energy 9 J. A perpendicular force does zero work on the instantaneous displacement.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>Power is the rate of doing work. For a constant force, instantaneous power is the dot product F · v.</p>", "quiz": [["A 2 kg body moving at 3 m/s has kinetic energy:", ["3 J", "6 J", "9 J", "18 J"], 2, "K = ½mv² = ½ × 2 × 9 = 9 J."], ["A constant 10 N force acts along a displacement of 2 m. Its work is:", ["5 J", "12 J", "20 J", "0 J"], 2, "W = Fs cos 0° = 10 × 2 = 20 J."], ["The SI unit of power is:", ["Joule", "Newton", "Watt", "Pascal"], 2, "One watt is one joule per second."]]}, {"subject": "Physics", "grade": 12, "title": "Electric charges and fields", "chapter": "Electric charges and fields", "keywords": "coulomb electrostatics electric charge", "body": "<h3>Core idea</h3><p>The magnitude of the electrostatic force between two point charges is F = k|q₁q₂|/r². Like charges repel and unlike charges attract. Electric field is force per unit positive test charge.</p><h3>Worked connection</h3><p>Doubling the separation while keeping charges fixed makes the force one-quarter of its original value.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>Coulomb’s law in this form assumes point charges, or spherical charge distributions with suitable symmetry, in a uniform medium.</p>", "quiz": [["The separation of two point charges doubles. Their force magnitude becomes:", ["Twice", "Half", "One-quarter", "Four times"], 2, "The force varies as 1/r²; doubling r divides the force by four."], ["The SI unit of electric field is:", ["N/C", "C/N", "J/s", "C/s"], 0, "Electric field E = F/q has unit newton per coulomb."], ["Two positive point charges:", ["Attract", "Repel", "Have no force", "Always cancel each other"], 1, "Like charges repel electrostatically."]]}, {"subject": "Physics", "grade": 12, "title": "Ray optics: lenses", "chapter": "Ray optics: lenses", "keywords": "optics focal length lens dioptre", "body": "<h3>Core idea</h3><p>For a thin lens with the Cartesian sign convention, 1/f = 1/v − 1/u. Lens power P = 1/f when f is measured in metres. A converging lens has positive power.</p><h3>Worked connection</h3><p>A converging lens with focal length +0.50 m has power +2 D. A diverging lens with focal length −0.25 m has power −4 D.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>Use a consistent sign convention. A real object placed beyond 2f of a converging lens forms a real, inverted, diminished image between f and 2f.</p>", "quiz": [["A converging lens has focal length +0.50 m. Its power is:", ["+0.5 D", "+2 D", "−2 D", "+50 D"], 1, "P = 1/f = 1/0.50 = +2 dioptres."], ["For a real object beyond 2f of a converging lens, the image is:", ["Real, inverted and diminished", "Virtual and enlarged", "Real and upright", "At infinity"], 0, "It is formed between f and 2f and is real, inverted and smaller."], ["A diverging lens with f = −0.25 m has power:", ["+4 D", "−0.25 D", "−4 D", "+0.25 D"], 2, "P = 1/(−0.25) = −4 D."]]}, {"subject": "Chemistry", "grade": 12, "title": "Solutions and concentration", "chapter": "Solutions and concentration", "keywords": "molarity molality solvent solute", "body": "<h3>Core idea</h3><p>Molarity is moles of solute per litre of solution. Molality is moles of solute per kilogram of solvent. The denominator matters: solution volume differs from solvent mass.</p><h3>Worked connection</h3><p>Dissolving 0.5 mol of solute and making the final solution volume 2 L gives 0.25 mol/L.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>Molarity can change with temperature because volume changes. Molality is based on mass and does not depend on thermal expansion.</p>", "quiz": [["0.5 mol of solute in 2 L of solution has molarity:", ["0.25 M", "1 M", "2.5 M", "4 M"], 0, "M = n/V = 0.5/2 = 0.25 mol/L."], ["Molality uses which denominator?", ["Litres of solution", "Kilograms of solvent", "Moles of solvent", "Grams of solution"], 1, "Molality = moles of solute / mass of solvent in kilograms."], ["Which concentration is unaffected by thermal expansion at fixed composition?", ["Molarity", "Molality", "Mass per volume", "Moles per litre"], 1, "Molality uses masses, which do not change through thermal expansion."]]}, {"subject": "Chemistry", "grade": 12, "title": "Electrochemistry foundations", "chapter": "Electrochemistry foundations", "keywords": "anode cathode redox galvanic cell", "body": "<h3>Core idea</h3><p>Oxidation occurs at the anode and reduction at the cathode. In a galvanic cell, a spontaneous redox reaction supplies electrical energy. Electrons flow through the external circuit from anode to cathode.</p><h3>Worked connection</h3><p>Using standard reduction potentials, E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode. Values +0.34 V and −0.76 V yield E°cell = +1.10 V.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>The anode is negative in a galvanic cell but positive in an electrolytic cell. Define electrodes by reaction, not by a universal sign.</p>", "quiz": [["Oxidation occurs at the:", ["Cathode", "Anode", "Salt bridge only", "Voltmeter"], 1, "The anode is defined as the electrode where oxidation occurs."], ["In a galvanic cell, external electrons flow from:", ["Cathode to anode", "Anode to cathode", "Salt bridge to cathode", "Solution to voltmeter only"], 1, "Oxidation releases electrons at the anode; reduction consumes them at the cathode."], ["Standard reduction potentials are +0.34 V (cathode) and −0.76 V (anode). E°cell is:", ["−1.10 V", "−0.42 V", "+0.42 V", "+1.10 V"], 3, "E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V."]]}, {"subject": "Chemistry", "grade": 12, "title": "Chemical kinetics: first order", "chapter": "Chemical kinetics: first order", "keywords": "rate constant half life reaction", "body": "<h3>Core idea</h3><p>For a first-order reaction, rate = k[A]. Its integrated rate law is ln([A]₀/[A]t) = kt, and half-life t½ = ln 2/k. The rate constant has units of inverse time.</p><h3>Worked connection</h3><p>A first-order reaction with k = 0.693 min⁻¹ has half-life 1 min. After two half-lives, one-quarter of the starting reactant remains.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>Reaction order is determined experimentally. It cannot generally be read from the balanced equation.</p>", "quiz": [["The unit of a first-order rate constant, using seconds, is:", ["mol/L", "s⁻¹", "L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹", "mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹"], 1, "Rate/[A] has units s⁻¹ for a first-order process."], ["A first-order reactant remaining after two half-lives is:", ["75%", "50%", "25%", "0%"], 2, "The fraction remaining is (½)² = ¼."], ["For first-order kinetics, half-life depends on:", ["Initial concentration only", "The rate constant", "Container colour", "Product mass only"], 1, "t½ = ln 2/k, independent of initial concentration."]]}, {"subject": "Biology", "grade": 11, "title": "Cell cycle and division", "chapter": "Cell cycle and division", "keywords": "mitosis meiosis dna cell division", "body": "<h3>Core idea</h3><p>The cell cycle includes interphase and the division phase. DNA replication occurs in S phase. Mitosis usually preserves chromosome number. Meiosis reduces chromosome number and contributes to genetic variation.</p><h3>Worked connection</h3><p>A diploid cell with 2n = 8 produces meiotic products with n = 4, under normal segregation.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>Crossing over occurs during pachytene of prophase I. Homologous chromosomes separate in anaphase I, while sister chromatids separate in anaphase II.</p>", "quiz": [["DNA replication occurs during:", ["G₁ phase", "S phase", "G₂ phase only", "Anaphase"], 1, "The S (synthesis) phase is when DNA is replicated."], ["A diploid cell has 2n = 8. Its normal meiotic products have:", ["2 chromosomes", "4 chromosomes", "8 chromosomes", "16 chromosomes"], 1, "Meiosis halves the chromosome number to n = 4."], ["Homologous chromosomes separate during:", ["Anaphase I", "Prophase I", "Metaphase II", "Interphase"], 0, "Anaphase I separates homologous chromosomes; anaphase II separates sister chromatids."]]}, {"subject": "Biology", "grade": 12, "title": "DNA and genetic information", "chapter": "DNA and genetic information", "keywords": "molecular inheritance dna nucleotide base", "body": "<h3>Core idea</h3><p>DNA is composed of nucleotide units containing a sugar, phosphate and nitrogenous base. In typical double-stranded DNA, adenine pairs with thymine, and guanine pairs with cytosine. The strands are antiparallel.</p><h3>Worked connection</h3><p>If double-stranded DNA contains 20% adenine, it contains 20% thymine and 30% each of guanine and cytosine.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>RNA usually contains ribose and uracil instead of deoxyribose and thymine. DNA replication is semiconservative: each daughter duplex has one parental and one newly synthesized strand.</p>", "quiz": [["In typical double-stranded DNA, adenine pairs with:", ["Uracil", "Guanine", "Thymine", "Cytosine"], 2, "A pairs with T; G pairs with C."], ["Double-stranded DNA contains 20% adenine. Its guanine percentage is:", ["20%", "30%", "40%", "60%"], 1, "T = 20%; G + C = 60%; G = C = 30%."], ["Semiconservative DNA replication produces duplexes with:", ["Two old strands", "Two new strands only", "One old and one new strand", "No parental material"], 2, "Each daughter duplex retains one parental strand paired with a new strand."]]}, {"subject": "Biology", "grade": 12, "title": "Ecosystems and energy flow", "chapter": "Ecosystems and energy flow", "keywords": "ecology trophic food chain pyramid", "body": "<h3>Core idea</h3><p>Producers capture energy and support consumers. Energy flows through trophic levels and is dissipated as heat. Nutrients cycle, but energy is not recycled through ecosystems in the same way.</p><h3>Worked connection</h3><p>Using the approximate 10% transfer rule, 10,000 J at a producer level supports about 1,000 J at the primary-consumer level and 100 J at the secondary-consumer level.</p><h3>Watch out</h3><p>The 10% rule is a teaching approximation, not a universal exact value. Pyramids of energy are always upright when expressed per unit area per unit time.</p>", "quiz": [["Using the approximate 10% rule, 1,000 J at producers gives primary consumers about:", ["10 J", "100 J", "1,000 J", "10,000 J"], 1, "About 10% of 1,000 J is 100 J."], ["Which ecological pyramid is always upright?", ["Numbers", "Biomass", "Energy", "All pyramids"], 2, "Energy decreases between successive trophic levels."], ["Which statement is correct?", ["Energy and nutrients both cycle identically", "Energy flows; nutrients cycle", "Nutrients never cycle", "All energy reaches top consumers"], 1, "Energy dissipates as heat while materials are recycled through biogeochemical processes."]]}]);
